WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.680
There are a lot of clever moments and uncomfortable
subtext in the original Back to the Future

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.480
that flew right over your head if you watched
it when you were a kid.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.960
From rock and roll nods to Freudian subplots,
here are things that only adults notice in

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:12.960
Back to the Future.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:17.440
As strange as it might be for a cool rock
‘n’ roll skateboard teen to form a lasting

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:22.100
friendship with a middle-aged science weirdo,
the emotional core of Back to the Future is

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.880
definitely Marty McFly’s relationship with
Doc Brown.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:29.520
But believe it or not, the relationship that
likely inspired Marty and Doc’s friendship

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:30.620
is even weirder.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.640
Marty’s out-of-his-depth impulsiveness and
Doc’s blunt knowledge bear a striking resemblance

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:40.490
to Mr. Peabody and Sherman, the stars of a
segment from the Rocky &amp; Bullwinkle show about

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.460
a genius talking dog and his young friend
traveling through time.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.540
"What is it?"

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:46.700
"Actually, it’s a time machine."

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:52.440
If that connection seems like a stretch, consider
that when Marty travels back to 1955, the

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.040
first thing he does is crash into a farmhouse
owned by the Peabody family, and while he’s

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.520
not named in the film, the credits identify
the boy who assumes he’s an alien as Sherman.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.200
It’s a fun reference, but maybe it’s also
the reason why the actual first time traveler

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:09.180
in the film is Einstein, Doc Brown’s faithful
dog.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:13.860
One of the most appealing parts of the movie,
especially to children, is Marty getting to

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.720
meet his parents when they were his age.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:21.320
Instead of inscrutable authority figures,
Marty's parents in 1955 are his awkward, goofy

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:21.820
peers.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.760
Seeing them at that age isn’t just weird
for Marty, it reminds the younger audience

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.660
that their parents were young once too.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.560
Less wholesome, especially to adults, are
the extremely uncomfortable scenes in which

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.500
Marty's mother tries to seduce him.

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:39.440
The incestuous subtext of Marty having to
fend off his mother’s advances, and the

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.740
fact that her crush on him is enough to threaten
the entire timeline could keep Sigmund Freud

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:44.939
busy for years.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.300
"It's like I'm kissing my brother."

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:53.999
Back to the Future is great for a lot of reasons,
but the climactic musical performance that

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.340
Marty puts on at the prom is a highlight in
a movie full of them.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.040
It brings character beats and off-hand lines
full circle as Marty finally gets to play

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:06.600
on stage in front of his whole high school,
although about 30 years earlier than he hoped,

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:10.560
while assuring that his parents get together…
and that he gets to keep existing.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.660
It's a great scene, packed full of great references...

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.340
"Your cousin Marvin Berry!

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.800
Most fans got the joke that Chuck Berry's
cousin calls him to let him know about "Johnny

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:25.830
B. Goode," but Marty also emulates Eddie Van
Halen, Jimi Hendrix, and Angus Young from

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.880
AC/DC, touches added by Michael J. Fox.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:33.360
That said, as a grown-up, you might think
it’s a little weird for this movie to suggest

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.930
that rock ‘n’ roll was actually invented
by a white suburban teen and then copied by

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:39.400
black artists in the ‘50s.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.000
That’s pretty weird, right?

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.300
Marty really gets put through the wringer
trying to get his parents to hook up after

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.500
he accidentally interrupts their meet cute
in 1955.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.680
One of the most inventive tricks he pulls
is to pretend to be an alien and threaten

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.980
George with death if he doesn't ask Lorraine
out.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.540
Since Marty knows that George is a bit of
a science fiction geek, he announces his fake

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.140
alien persona with some pretty familiar names.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.680
"I am an extraterrestrial from the planet
Vulcan!"

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:10.400
By the end of the movie, George has even written
a novel that, based on the cover design, is

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.459
at least partly inspired by the meeting.

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.000
It’s a cute gag, but in the world of Back
to the Future, did George start writing about

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.620
Darth Vader and Vulcan in the ‘50s, years
before Star Wars and Star Trek were released?

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.239
Does the McFly family’s new prosperity come
from a lawsuit against George Lucas claiming

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.340
copyright infringement from George’s work?

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:33.120
And if not, what did he think when two of
the most popular sci-fi stories started referencing

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.880
his own alien encounter?

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:40.420
It's pretty easy to imagine adult George McFly
attending sci-fi conventions trying to snag

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:44.810
a few precious seconds with Lucas and Gene
Roddenberry to confirm they were visited by

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.080
this strange alien, too.

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.159
While it seems like a happy ending, the final
moments of Back to the Future are much darker

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:53.000
than is apparent at first glance.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:57.200
Marty's gotten the truck of his dreams, his
brother is wearing a suit to work instead

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.820
of slacking off at home, and his mom and dad
seem a whole lot happier and much more well-off.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:06.359
The thing is, Marty comes from the timeline
that we see back at the beginning of the film,

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:10.310
and he has strong memories of what his family
used to be like before he started messing

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:11.980
around with the space-time continuum.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.840
If you think about it for a moment, it becomes
existentially horrifying when you remember

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.960
that Marty doesn't really know these people.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:23.810
They look like his family and act like an
improved version of his family, but they reference

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:27.970
moments that never happened to this Marty,
leaving him confused when he compares them

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.840
to the people he left just yesterday in 1985.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:36.640
Imagine having an entire life that's similar,
but decidedly different from the one you remember.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.000
Imagine sitting down with your family as they
make references to events from your childhood

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:45.730
that are just close enough to leave you with
deja vu but not enough to spur any actual

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.620
memories of the event in question.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:53.000
Marty's old family, with all their problems
and personality flaws, are pretty much dead,

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.480
replaced by strangers who have had entire
lives that he can’t remember.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:01.340
It’s amazing that Back to the Future 2 didn’t
take place entirely in a therapist’s office.

